 when there are multiple  categories of ads when the fairness uh  definitions uh should look a little  different  and then we also in addition to these  theoretical results provide experiments  on the yahoo data set um and  uh  show that illustrate the performance of  um our family of ad auctions  okay so for the remainder of the talk  i'm going to be focusing on this uh the  these value stable auctions that we  design and the properties that they have  okay so let's just kind of recall what  the value stability condition says so  essentially recall that it if it  requires that if two value vectors are  multiplicatively close then the  allocation vectors need to be additively  close in particular their difference  needs to be bounded by f of lambda and  this is kind of an l infinity condition  on the allocation vectors we require  this um for every advertiser  um and we measure the performance of our  allocation algorithms uh by measuring  the social welfare in comparison to the  unfair optimal so this is uh basically  we sum over all users look at the social  welfare uh obtained on each user and  then divide by this um benchmark which  is kind of the sum over users of the  maximum value that any advertiser places  on that user  so the goal here is to design value  stable allocation algorithms that  achieve a near optimal approximation  ratio  okay so essentially what value stability  is requiring is some kind of a  smoothness condition so uh recall that  the highest bid wins auction kind of  exaggerated these small differences in  the bids um value stability is  essentially requiring that um  these uh sharp forms of exaggeration um  and disallowing these sharp forms of  exaggeration  okay so a first attempt at defining um  value stability or value stable at  auctions would be proportional  allocation so the idea here is just set  the allocation proportional to the  values so um  the allocation here um for a given  advertiser is how much that advertiser  values uh the user divided by um the sum  of how much all of the uh  each of the advertisers values that user  so it's uh not difficult to show that  value stability does hold for um  proportional allocation  with respect to this function f of  lambda equals one minus lambda to the  negative one which was in that family of  value stability constraints that i  mentioned before  however the main problem with  proportional allocation is the  approximation ratio  so the problem here is that the  approximation ratio actually goes to  zero as the number of advertisers goes  to infinity  so to see that um consider a value  vector with one high bid and then a  bunch or one high value and then a bunch  of small values  so we see here that essentially as k  goes to infinity the allocation that the  um proportional allocation places on the  value one is going to zero so as a  result this approximation ratio is going  to zero  so this problem actually is uh persists  for any allocation rule that allocates  proportionally to a function of the  value so this is even if we sort of  consider a more general class of  allocation algorithms where we take a  function of values and then allocate  proportionally to the function of the  values we'd still have this problem as k  goes to infinity  so um  this sort of demonstrates that to get uh  to to get a good approximation ratio  when the number of advertisers is large  we need to move outside of the family of  proportional allocation algorithms  so our main contribution from an  algorithmic perspective is this family  of inverse proportional allocation um  algorithms that um sort of addresses  this problem  so the core idea here is to rather than  uh allocating proportionally to the  values deduct proportionally to the  inverse of the values  so start by allocating  the ad slot to every user and then  deduct proportionally  to the inverse of the values and keep  kind of deducting until the sum of the  allocations across all advertisers is  one  so